{
      "order": 2296,
      "label": "OPT5",
      "kind": "A",
      "category": "numerical-optimization-inverse-problems-and-uncertainty-quantification",
      "id": "inequality-kkt-duality-and-optimality-certificates",
      "title": "Inequality kkt duality and optimality certificates",
      "companion": "inequality-kkt-duality-and-optimality-certificates-examples",
      "requires": [
        "constant-rank-submersions-and-regular-level-sets",
        "locally-convex-spaces-and-continuous-separation",
        "jacobi-fields-conjugate-points-and-the-cut-locus"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-inequality-constrained-program-and-kkt-geometry",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Inequality constrained programs, tangent cones, constraint qualifications, and KKT",
          "deps": [
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient"
          ],
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$\nbe $C^2$. Let $p,m\\in\\mathbb N$ with zero allowed; when the target\ndimension is positive, let $h:U\\to\\mathbb R^p$ and\n$g=(g_1,\\ldots,g_m):U\\to\\mathbb R^m$ be componentwise $C^2$, and when it\nis zero use the unique empty map. The $C^2$ convention for positive\ndimensions is [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]].\nThe smooth inequality program is to minimize $f(x)$ subject to\n$h(x)=0$ and $g_i(x)\\le0$ for every $i$. Its feasible set is\n$C=\\{x\\in U:h(x)=0,\\ g_i(x)\\le0\\ (1\\le i\\le m)\\}$. A feasible point $x_*$ is\na local minimizer if some open neighborhood $V$ of $x_*$ satisfies\n$f(x_*)\\le f(y)$ for every $y\\in C\\cap V$.\n\nAt a feasible $x$, the active set is\n$I(x)=\\{i:g_i(x)=0\\}$. The Bouligand tangent cone and linearized cone are,\nrespectively,\n$$\nT_C(x)=\\{d:\\exists\\,t_k>0,\\ t_k\\downarrow0,\\ d_k\\to d,\\ x+t_kd_k\\in C\\},\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\mathcal L_C(x)=\\{d:Dh(x)d=0,\\ Dg_i(x)d\\le0\\text{ for every }i\\in I(x)\\}.\n$$\nGradients are column vectors and $Dg_i(x)d=\\nabla g_i(x)^Td$.\n\nThe linear independence constraint qualification (LICQ) holds at $x$ when the rows of $Dh(x)$ together with\n$\\{\\nabla g_i(x)^T:i\\in I(x)\\}$ are linearly independent. MFCQ holds at $x$\nwhen $Dh(x)$ has full row rank (its rows are linearly independent) and there is $w\\in\\ker Dh(x)$ with\n$Dg_i(x)w<0$ for every active $i$; the latter condition is vacuous if\n$I(x)=\\varnothing$. Here full row rank for a matrix with zero rows is\nunderstood vacuously.\n\nThe Mangasarian–Fromovitz constraint qualification (MFCQ) is the condition\njust stated. The Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT) system is the stationarity,\nfeasibility, dual-feasibility, and complementarity system below.\n\nFor $\\nu\\in\\mathbb R^p$ and $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R^m$, the Lagrangian and KKT\nsystem are\n$$\nL(x,\\nu,\\lambda)=f(x)+\\nu^Th(x)+\\lambda^Tg(x),\n$$\n$$\nh(x)=0,\\quad g(x)\\le0,\\quad \\lambda\\ge0,\\quad \\lambda_i g_i(x)=0\\ (1\\le i\\le m),\\quad \\nabla_xL(x,\\nu,\\lambda)=0.\n$$\nThe critical cone for a KKT tuple is\n$\\mathcal K(x,\\nu,\\lambda)=\\mathcal L_C(x)\\cap\n\\{d:\\nabla f(x)^Td=0\\}$. These definitions use the differentiability and\ngradient conventions of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]],\n[[def-euclidean-inner-product]], and [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]].",
          "strategy": "Definition only; no phase proof is applicable.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Nocedal and Wright, Numerical Optimization, second edition",
                "url": "https://www.ime.unicamp.br/~pulino/MT404/TextosOnline/NocedalJ.pdf",
                "locator": "Chapter 12, Sections 12.1–12.3 and 12.5, printed pp. 317–341 and 351–353; conventions translated from constraints c_i(x) >= 0 to g_i(x) <= 0."
              },
              {
                "title": "Boyd and Vandenberghe, Convex Optimization",
                "url": "https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/bv_cvxbook.pdf",
                "locator": "Chapter 5, Sections 5.1–5.2, printed pp. 219–227."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "def-convex-program-duality-and-slater-condition",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite-dimensional convex program, Lagrangian dual, and Slater condition",
          "deps": [
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space"
          ],
          "statement": "A subset is convex when it contains the segment joining each pair of its\npoints ([[def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space]]). A function\n$F:\\mathbb R^n\\to(-\\infty,+\\infty]$ is proper if it never equals\n$-\\infty$ and is finite at at least one point. Its domain is\n$\\operatorname{dom}F=\\{x:F(x)<+\\infty\\}$. Its epigraph is\n$\\operatorname{epi}F=\\{(x,t):F(x)\\le t\\}$. It is closed convex when its\nepigraph is a closed convex set. For a nonempty convex set $S$,\n$\\operatorname{aff}S$ is its smallest containing affine subspace and\n$\\operatorname{ri}S$ is its interior relative to $\\operatorname{aff}S$.\n\nLet $n\\ge1$, let $m,p\\in\\mathbb N$ with zero allowed, let\n$f_0,g_1,\\ldots,g_m$ be proper closed convex functions on $\\mathbb R^n$,\nlet $A\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times n}$ have full row rank, and let\n$b\\in\\mathbb R^p$. Full row rank means that the rows are linearly\nindependent, vacuously when $p=0$. The common domain is\n$D=\\operatorname{dom}f_0\\cap\\bigcap_{i=1}^m\\operatorname{dom}g_i$. The primal\nproblem is to minimize $f_0(x)$ over $x\\in D$ satisfying $Ax=b$ and\n$g_i(x)\\le0$. An inequality is removable if, with\n$D_{-i}=\\operatorname{dom}f_0\\cap\\bigcap_{j\\ne i}\\operatorname{dom}g_j$,\n$D_{-i}\\subseteq\\operatorname{dom}g_i$ and $g_i(x)\\le0$ for every $x\\in D_{-i}$.\nThen $D=D_{-i}$, so removing that function and its inequality leaves the\nfeasible set unchanged. Remove such inequalities one at a time, recomputing\n$D_{-i}$ after each deletion. An inequality that is nonpositive only on $D$\nbut whose function domain restricts $D$ is not removable. Unless stated\notherwise, the inequalities below are the remaining ones after only these\ndomain-preserving deletions. The primal value is\n$$\np^*=\\inf\\{f_0(x):x\\in D,\\ Ax=b,\\ g_i(x)\\le0\\text{ for all }i\\}.\n$$\n\nFor $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R^m_+$ and $\\nu\\in\\mathbb R^p$, the extended\nLagrangian is\n$$\nL(x,\\lambda,\\nu)=\\begin{cases}f_0(x)+\\lambda^Tg(x)+\\nu^T(Ax-b),&x\\in D,\\\\+\\infty,&x\\notin D.\\end{cases}\n$$\nThe dual function and dual value are\n$$\nq(\\lambda,\\nu)=\\inf_{x\\in\\mathbb R^n}L(x,\\lambda,\\nu),\\qquad d^*=\\sup_{\\lambda\\ge0,\\ \\nu\\in\\mathbb R^p}q(\\lambda,\\nu).\n$$\nThe dual supremum is attained when some admissible $(\\lambda,\\nu)$ has\n$q(\\lambda,\\nu)=d^*$; the optimal duality gap is $p^*-d^*$ when both values\nare finite.\n\nSlater's condition means that there is\n$x_s\\in\\operatorname{ri}(\\operatorname{dom}f_0)\\cap\n\\bigcap_i\\operatorname{ri}(\\operatorname{dom}g_i)$ with $Ax_s=b$ and\n$g_i(x_s)<0$ for every remaining inequality. Relative interior is used in\nthe affine hull of each domain, so the domain need not have ambient interior.",
          "strategy": "Definition only; no phase proof is applicable.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Boyd and Vandenberghe, Convex Optimization",
                "url": "https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/bv_cvxbook.pdf",
                "locator": "Chapter 5, Sections 5.1–5.3.2, printed pp. 215–236; primal and dual values, weak duality, relative-interior Slater condition and dual attainment."
              },
              {
                "title": "Zhiyuan Li, Convex Optimization, Lecture 5: Lagrange Multipliers and KKT",
                "url": "https://zhiyuanli.ttic.edu/convex-optimization-site/Lecture-5___-Lagrange-Multipliers-and-KKT/",
                "locator": "Sections 5.1–5.2, Definitions 5.1 and 5.4 and Theorem 5.3 with its full perturbation-value-function proof."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-finitely-generated-euclidean-cones-are-closed",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "A finitely generated cone in Euclidean space is closed",
          "deps": [
            "cor-finite-dimensional-subspaces-are-closed",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm"
          ],
          "statement": "For any finite list $a_1,\\ldots,a_m\\in\\mathbb R^n$, including the empty\nlist, the cone\n$$\nK=\\left\\{\\sum_{j=1}^m t_j a_j:t_j\\ge0\\right\\}\n$$\nis a closed convex subset of $\\mathbb R^n$.",
          "strategy": "1.1 Every $y\\in K$ has a representation whose positive-coefficient vectors are linearly independent. Start with any nonnegative coefficient list and discard its zero coefficients. If its remaining vectors are dependent, take a nonzero relation $\\sum_j c_j a_j=0$ and change its sign so some $c_j>0$. For the current positive coefficients $t_j$, put $s=\\min_{c_j>0}t_j/c_j$. Replacing each $t_j$ by $t_j-sc_j$ preserves the represented vector, leaves every coefficient nonnegative, and makes at least one positive coefficient zero. Repeating at most $m$ times leaves an independent positive support. [given, choose, algebra]\n\n1.2 Fix an independent index set $J$ and let $A_J$ have columns $a_j$, $j\\in J$. Its Gram matrix $G_J=A_J^TA_J$ is invertible: for every nonzero $v$, $v^TG_Jv=\\|A_Jv\\|_2^2>0$ by independence. Put $B_J=G_J^{-1}A_J^T$, define $R_J(y)=B_Jy$ on $\\mathbb R^n$, and set $L_J=(\\sum_{r=1}^{|J|}\\sum_{s=1}^n(B_J)_{rs}^2)^{1/2}$. Applying [F2] to each row gives $\\|R_J(y)-R_J(y')\\|_2^2\\le L_J^2\\|y-y'\\|_2^2$, so $R_J$ is Lipschitz with finite constant $L_J$, hence continuous. On the closed subspace $S_J=\\operatorname{span}\\{a_j:j\\in J\\}$ it satisfies $A_JR_J(y)=y$. Hence $A_J(\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}^{|J|})= S_J\\cap R_J^{-1}(\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}^{|J|})$ is closed: $S_J$ is closed by [F1], the nonnegative orthant is closed, and $R_J$ is continuous. For $J=\\varnothing$ this cone is $\\{0\\}$, also closed. [given, F1, F2, algebra]\n\n2.1 By step 1.1, $K$ is the union of the cones in step 1.2 over independent subsets $J\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$. There are only finitely many such subsets, so this union is closed. Each component is a convex cone, and directly taking nonnegative combinations shows $K$ is convex. [step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎",
