          "statement": "Let $A\\in\\mathbb R^{r\\times n}$ and $b\\in\\mathbb R^r$. Exactly one of the\nfollowing holds:\n\n1. There is $x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ with $x\\ge0$ and $Ax=b$.\n2. There is $y\\in\\mathbb R^r$ with $A^Ty\\ge0$ and $b^Ty<0$.\n\nInequalities between vectors are coordinatewise.",
          "strategy": "1.1 Let $C=\\{\\sum_{j=1}^n x_j A_j:x_j\\ge0\\}$, where $A_j$ are the columns of $A$. This is a nonempty convex cone and is closed by [F1]. If $b\\in C$, its nonnegative coefficients give alternative 1. If $b\\notin C$, let $p$ be its nearest point in $C$ by [F2] and put $z=b-p\\ne0$. [F1, F2, given, construct]\n\n2.1 Applying [F3] with $c=0$ and $c=2p$ gives both $\\langle z,p\\rangle\\ge0$ and $\\langle z,p\\rangle\\le0$, hence $\\langle z,p\\rangle=0$. Applying [F3] to arbitrary $c\\in C$ then gives $\\langle z,c\\rangle\\le0$. In particular $A_j^Tz\\le0$ for every column, and $\\langle z,b\\rangle=\\langle z,p+z\\rangle=\\|z\\|_2^2>0$. Thus $y=-z$ satisfies $A^Ty\\ge0$ and $b^Ty=-\\|z\\|_2^2<0$, which is alternative 2. [F3, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 The alternatives cannot both hold: if $Ax=b$ with $x\\ge0$ and $A^Ty\\ge0$, then $b^Ty=x^TA^Ty\\ge0$, contradicting $b^Ty<0$. Step 1.1 produces one alternative in every case, so exactly one holds. [step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
