          "strategy": "1.1 Every $d\\in T_C(x_*)$ satisfies $\\nabla f(x_*)^Td\\ge0$. For $d=0$ this is immediate. Otherwise take a defining sequence $x_*+t_kd_k\\in C$, with $t_k\\downarrow0$ and $d_k\\to d$. For large $k$, local minimality gives $f(x_*+t_kd_k)-f(x_*)\\ge0$. Differentiability gives $f(x_*+t_kd_k)-f(x_*)=t_k\\nabla f(x_*)^Td_k+o(t_k)$; divide by $t_k$ and pass to the limit. [given, algebra]\n\n2.1 By [F1] and step 1.1, $\\nabla f(x_*)^Td\\ge0$ for every $d\\in\\mathcal L_C(x_*)$. Form a matrix $B$ with columns $\\nabla h_j(x_*),-\\nabla h_j(x_*)$ for each equality and $-\\nabla g_i(x_*)$ for each active inequality, and set $c=\\nabla f(x_*)$. If alternative 1 of [F2] failed, alternative 2 would give $B^Td\\ge0$ and $c^Td<0$. The paired equality columns force $Dh(x_*)d=0$, while the inequality columns force $Dg_i(x_*)d\\le0$ for every active $i$, so $d\\in\\mathcal L_C(x_*)$, contradicting step 1.1. Thus there are nonnegative coefficients representing $c$ as a combination of the columns of $B$. [F1, F2, step 1.1, construct]\n\n3.1 Write the equality-column coefficients as $\\alpha_j,\\beta_j\\ge0$ and the active inequality coefficients as $\\lambda_i\\ge0$. The representation from step 2.1 is $\\nabla f(x_*)=Dh(x_*)^T(\\alpha-\\beta)-Dg_{I(x_*)}(x_*)^T\\lambda_I$. Set $\\nu=-(\\alpha-\\beta)$ and set $\\lambda_i=0$ for inactive $i$. Then stationarity holds. Feasibility holds because $x_*\\in C$; dual feasibility holds by construction; and complementarity holds because active constraints vanish while inactive multipliers are zero. These are exactly the KKT conditions. [step 2.1, given, algebra] ∎",
