          "statement": "For the $C^2$ program of\n[[def-inequality-constrained-program-and-kkt-geometry]], let $x_*$ be a\nlocal minimizer satisfying LICQ, and let $(\\nu,\\lambda)$ be its KKT\nmultipliers. Then\n$$\nd^T\\nabla_{xx}^2L(x_*,\\nu,\\lambda)d\\ge0\\qquad\\text{for every }d\\in\\mathcal K(x_*,\\nu,\\lambda).\n$$",
          "strategy": "1.1 If $d=0$ the result is immediate, so assume $d\\ne0$. Let $P=\\{i\\in I(x_*):\\lambda_i>0\\}$ and $Z=\\{i\\in I(x_*):\\lambda_i=0,\\ Dg_i(x_*)d=0\\}$. Put $H=(h,(g_i)_{i\\in P})$. By LICQ the rows of $DH(x_*)$, together with the rows $Dg_i(x_*)$ for $i\\in Z$, are independent. Thus the combined finite-dimensional linear map is onto. Choose $z$ solving $ Dh(x_*)z+D^2h(x_*)[d,d]=0,\\quad Dg_i(x_*)z+D^2g_i(x_*)[d,d]=0\\ (i\\in P), $ and $ Dg_i(x_*)z+D^2g_i(x_*)[d,d]=-1\\ (i\\in Z). $ The first two groups say $DH(x_*)z+D^2H(x_*)[d,d]=0$. [given, F1, F2, construct]\n\n2.1 By [F3] there is a $C^2$ curve $\\gamma$ with $H(\\gamma(t))=H(x_*)$, $\\gamma(0)=x_*$, $\\gamma'(0)=d$, and $\\gamma''(0)=z$. For $i\\in P$ the corresponding active inequality is exactly fixed at zero. An active $i\\notin P$ has $\\lambda_i=0$; by [F2], either $Dg_i(x_*)d<0$, giving $g_i(\\gamma(t))=tDg_i(x_*)d+O(t^2)<0$, or $i\\in Z$, giving $g_i(\\gamma(t))=-t^2/2+o(t^2)<0$. Inactive inequalities remain strict by continuity. Hence $\\gamma(t)$ is feasible for all sufficiently small $t>0$. [step 1.1, F2, F3, F4, algebra]\n\n2.2 Taylor expansion [F4], criticality $\\nabla f(x_*)^Td=0$, and $\\gamma(t)=x_*+td+\\tfrac12t^2z+o(t^2)$ give $ f(\\gamma(t))-f(x_*)= \\tfrac12t^2\\bigl(\\nabla f(x_*)^Tz+ d^T\\nabla^2 f(x_*)d\\bigr)+o(t^2). $ Stationarity gives $\\nabla f(x_*)^Tz=-\\nu^TDh(x_*)z-\\sum_{i\\in P}\\lambda_iDg_i(x_*)z$. The jet equations in step 1.1 therefore make the coefficient in parentheses equal to $d^T\\nabla_{xx}^2L(x_*,\\nu,\\lambda)d$. [F1, F2, step 1.1, F4, algebra]\n\n3.1 Local minimality gives $f(\\gamma(t))-f(x_*)\\ge0$ for small $t>0$. If the displayed Lagrangian quadratic form were negative, step 2.2 would make this difference negative for all sufficiently small positive $t$, a contradiction. Thus the quadratic form is nonnegative, as claimed. [step 2.1, step 2.2, given, assume-contra, discharge-contradiction] ∎",
