      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-failed-constraint-qualification-blocks-kkt",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "A true minimizer with no KKT multipliers when MFCQ fails",
          "deps": [
            "def-inequality-constrained-program-and-kkt-geometry",
            "thm-first-order-kkt-necessity-under-mfcq"
          ],
          "statement": "Minimize $f(x,y)=x$ subject to\n$g(x,y)=y^2-x^3\\le0$. The origin is a global minimizer, but it has no KKT\nmultiplier because the active constraint gradient vanishes there.",
          "strategy": "1.1 If $g(x,y)\\le0$, then $y^2\\le x^3$. Since $y^2\\ge0$, this forces $x^3\\ge0$, hence $x\\ge0$. The origin is feasible and has objective $0$, so it is a global minimizer. [given, algebra]\n\n1.2 At $(0,0)$ the constraint is active, $\\nabla f(0,0)=(1,0)$, and $\\nabla g(0,0)=(-3x^2,2y)|_{(0,0)}=(0,0)$. Thus $\\nabla f+\\lambda\\nabla g=(1,0)\\ne0$ for every $\\lambda\\ge0$; no KKT multiplier exists. MFCQ also fails because $Dg(0,0)w=0$ for every direction $w$, so no direction strictly decreases the active constraint. [given, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 This does not contradict [F2]: the origin is a local and global minimizer, but the MFCQ hypothesis fails, and the direct stationarity calculation proves multiplier existence cannot be inferred here. [step 1.1, step 1.2, F2] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Nocedal and Wright, Numerical Optimization, second edition",
                "url": "https://www.ime.unicamp.br/~pulino/MT404/TextosOnline/NocedalJ.pdf",
                "locator": "Chapter 12, Sections 12.2 and 12.5, printed pp. 328–329 and 351–353; constraint qualifications are required for first-order multiplier existence."
              },
              {
                "title": "Boyd and Vandenberghe, Convex Optimization",
                "url": "https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/bv_cvxbook.pdf",
                "locator": "Chapter 5, Sections 5.2–5.3, printed pp. 221–236; constraints qualifications and the separate Slater branch."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 3
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-closed-convex-program-with-positive-duality-gap",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "A closed convex program with a positive duality gap when Slater fails",
          "deps": [
            "def-convex-program-duality-and-slater-condition",
            "thm-lagrangian-weak-duality",
            "thm-slater-strong-duality-and-dual-attainment"
          ],
          "statement": "Define the extended-real perspective\n$$\n\\phi(x,y)=\\begin{cases}x^2/y,&y>0,\\\\0,&(x,y)=(0,0),\\\\+\\infty,&\\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}\n$$\nMinimize $f_0(x,y)=e^{-x}$ subject to $\\phi(x,y)\\le0$. Both functions are\nproper closed convex, the primal minimum is $1$, Slater fails, and the\nLagrangian dual value is $0$.",
          "strategy": "1.1 The epigraph of $\\phi$ is exactly $E=\\{(x,y,t):y\\ge0,\\ t\\ge0,\\ x^2\\le yt\\}$. For $y>0$ this is the inequality $t\\ge x^2/y$; when $y=0$ it forces $x=0$ and $t\\ge0$; for $y<0$ it is empty. Thus $E$ is closed, being defined by continuous non-strict inequalities. It is convex: if $|x_j|\\le\\sqrt{y_jt_j}$ for $j=1,2$, then for $0\\le\\theta\\le1$, $ |\\theta x_1+(1-\\theta)x_2| \\le\\theta\\sqrt{y_1t_1}+(1-\\theta)\\sqrt{y_2t_2} \\le\\sqrt{(\\theta y_1+(1-\\theta)y_2) (\\theta t_1+(1-\\theta)t_2)} $ by Cauchy–Schwarz. Therefore $\\phi$ is closed convex. The objective is continuous and has second derivative $e^{-x}>0$ in $x$, so it is closed convex and proper as well. [given, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 On its domain $\\phi\\ge0$. If $y>0$, $\\phi(x,y)\\le0$ forces $x=0$; if $y=0$, finiteness already forces $x=0$. Hence the feasible set is $\\{(0,y):y\\ge0\\}$ and $f_0=1$ throughout it, so $p^*=1$ and the primal minimum is attained, for example at $(0,0)$. No point has $\\phi(x,y)<0$, so Slater fails. [given, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 For $\\lambda\\ge0$, every point in the Lagrangian domain has $e^{-x}+\\lambda\\phi(x,y)\\ge0$, so $q(\\lambda)\\ge0$. At $\\lambda=0$, taking $x\\to+\\infty$ gives $q(0)=0$. For $\\lambda>0$, take $(x_k,y_k)=(k,k^3)$; then $e^{-x_k}+\\lambda\\phi(x_k,y_k)=e^{-k}+\\lambda/k\\to0$, so again $q(\\lambda)=0$. Therefore $d^*=\\sup_{\\lambda\\ge0}q(\\lambda)=0$ and the positive duality gap is $p^*-d^*=1$. This does not contradict [F2], because its Slater hypothesis fails. [F1, F2, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 The calculation gives a closed convex primal problem with attained finite optimum and a strict gap; the missing strict-feasibility condition is exactly the boundary that blocks the Slater theorem's certificate. [step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Zhiyuan Li, Convex Optimization, Lecture 5: Lagrange Multipliers and KKT",
                "url": "https://zhiyuanli.ttic.edu/convex-optimization-site/Lecture-5___-Lagrange-Multipliers-and-KKT/",
                "locator": "Example 5.5, lines 418–446; the source's open-domain perspective constraint has primal value 1 and dual value 0. This carrier adds its closed perspective boundary and verifies it independently."
              },
              {
                "title": "Boyd and Vandenberghe, Convex Optimization",
                "url": "https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/bv_cvxbook.pdf",
                "locator": "Chapter 5, Sections 5.2.3 and 5.3.2, printed pp. 226–236; Slater assumptions and strong-duality conclusion."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 3
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-active-set-quadratic-kkt-certificate",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "Active-set quadratic with an exact KKT certificate",
          "deps": [
            "def-inequality-constrained-program-and-kkt-geometry",
            "prop-licq-implies-mfcq-and-unique-kkt-multipliers",
            "thm-convex-kkt-sufficiency-and-normal-cone-certificate"
          ],
          "statement": "Minimize\n$$f(x_1,x_2)=\\tfrac12\\bigl((x_1+1)^2+(x_2-2)^2\\bigr)$$\nsubject to $g(x_1,x_2)=-x_1\\le0$. The point $x_*=(0,2)$ has one active\nconstraint and multiplier $\\lambda_*=1$.",
          "strategy": "1.1 The constraint is feasible exactly when $x_1\\ge0$. At $x_*=(0,2)$ it is active, and $\\nabla f(x_*)=(1,0)$ while $\\nabla g(x_*)=(-1,0)$. Therefore $\\nabla f(x_*)+\\lambda_*\\nabla g(x_*)=0$ for $\\lambda_*=1\\ge0$, and $\\lambda_*g(x_*)=0$. The active gradient is nonzero, so the one-row LICQ holds. [given, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 The quadratic is convex and differentiable. Primal feasibility and the multiplier checks in step 1.1 satisfy the hypotheses of [F2], so $x_*$ is a global minimizer. Directly, for every feasible $x$, $ f(x)-f(x_*)=x_1+\\tfrac12x_1^2+\\tfrac12(x_2-2)^2\\ge0, $ with equality only when $x_1=0$ and $x_2=2$. Thus the minimizer is unique, and [F1] makes $\\lambda_*=1$ the unique multiplier. [step 1.1, F1, F2, algebra]\n\n3.1 The active-set KKT certificate is exact: it proves the unique global minimizer $x_*=(0,2)$ and its unique nonnegative multiplier $\\lambda_*=1$. [step 1.1, step 2.1] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Nocedal and Wright, Numerical Optimization, second edition",
                "url": "https://www.ime.unicamp.br/~pulino/MT404/TextosOnline/NocedalJ.pdf",
                "locator": "Chapter 12, Sections 12.1–12.2, printed pp. 317–331; active inequalities and first-order KKT certificates."
              },
              {
                "title": "Boyd and Vandenberghe, Convex Optimization",
                "url": "https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/bv_cvxbook.pdf",
                "locator": "Chapter 5, Sections 5.1 and 5.5, KKT and global convex optimality certificates."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 4
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-kkt-stationary-saddle-point",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "An unconstrained KKT point that is a saddle",
          "deps": [
            "def-inequality-constrained-program-and-kkt-geometry",
            "thm-critical-cone-equivalence-under-kkt",
            "thm-second-order-necessary-condition-on-the-critical-cone",
            "thm-second-order-sufficient-condition-on-the-critical-cone"
          ],
          "statement": "With no constraints, let $f(x_1,x_2)=x_1^2-x_2^2$. The origin satisfies\nthe KKT stationarity equation, but is a saddle point rather than a local\nminimum.",
          "strategy": "1.1 The gradient is $\\nabla f(x_1,x_2)=(2x_1,-2x_2)$, so $\\nabla f(0,0)=0$ and the origin is KKT. With no constraints, $\\mathcal L_C(0)=\\mathbb R^2$ and $\\mathcal K(0)=\\mathbb R^2$. The Hessian is $\\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\\\0&-2\\end{pmatrix}$, whose quadratic form at $d=(0,1)$ is $-2$. [given, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 For every $t\\ne0$, $f(t,0)=t^2>0=f(0,0)$, while $f(0,t)=-t^2<0=f(0,0)$. Both points approach the origin as $t\\to0$, so the origin is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum. The negative critical curvature also violates the necessary condition in [F2], and strict positivity required by its sufficient condition fails. [given, F2, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Thus stationarity alone is not an optimality certificate: this KKT point is a saddle. [step 1.1, step 2.1] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Nocedal and Wright, Numerical Optimization, second edition",
                "url": "https://www.ime.unicamp.br/~pulino/MT404/TextosOnline/NocedalJ.pdf",
                "locator": "Chapter 12, Section 12.4, printed pp. 342–346; KKT and critical-cone second-order conditions."
              },
              {
                "title": "Boyd and Vandenberghe, Convex Optimization",
                "url": "https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/bv_cvxbook.pdf",
                "locator": "Chapter 4, local and global optimality; this saddle is a direct finite-dimensional calculation."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 5
        }
