        {
          "id": "lem-implicit-sensitivity-error-controlled-by-residual",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "An implicit sensitivity evaluated at an approximate state has a local residual bound",
          "deps": [
            "thm-euclidean-implicit-function-theorem",
            "thm-mean-value-inequality-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "statement": "Let $R(z,\\theta)$ be $C^2$ near an exact solution $R(z_*,\\theta_0)=0$, with $A(z)=R_z(z,\\theta_0)$ invertible and $B(z)=R_\\theta(z,\\theta_0)$. The implicit sensitivity at a state $z$ is $S(z)=-A(z)^{-1}B(z)$. Suppose the local inverse $\\Psi$ of $z\\mapsto R(z,\\theta_0)$, supplied near $z_*$ by the implicit function theorem, is defined on a convex residual neighborhood containing $0$ and $r=R(\\tilde z,\\theta_0)$, with $\\|D\\Psi(y)\\|\\le\\kappa$ along their segment. Suppose also that the segment from $z_*$ to $\\tilde z=\\Psi(r)$ lies in a convex neighborhood where $\\|A(z)^{-1}\\|\\le M$, $\\|B(z)\\|\\le B_0$, and $A,B$ are Lipschitz with constants $L_A,L_B$. Then\n$$\\|S(\\tilde z)-S(z_*)\\|\\le\\kappa\\bigl(M^2L_AB_0+ML_B\\bigr)\\|r\\|.$$\nThis bounds the implicit sensitivity formula evaluated at an approximate state. It is not by itself a bound for the derivative of a stopped iteration.",
          "strategy": "direct\n\n**Given:** The exact root, local inverse chart, residual, inverse-norm and Lipschitz bounds in the statement.\n\n[F1] [[thm-euclidean-implicit-function-theorem]]: if the state Jacobian of a $C^1$ residual is invertible at a zero, there is a local $C^1$ solution branch and its derivative formula; applying the theorem to $R(z,\\theta_0)-r=0$ gives the local inverse $\\Psi(r)$.\n\n[F2] [[thm-mean-value-inequality-for-total-derivatives]]: on a convex open set, a uniform derivative bound $\\|Df(z)v\\|\\le M\\|v\\|$ implies $\\|f(y)-f(x)\\|\\le M\\|y-x\\|$.\n\n[F3] [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]: the derivative of a composition is the composition of the derivatives at the corresponding points.\n\n[A1] The residual segment lies inside the inverse chart and the state segment lies inside the neighborhood where all stated bounds hold.",
          "sources": {
