          "dependency_level": 3,
          "statement": "For a finite scalar-output graph on one fixed strict branch, associate to each parent-slot edge $e$ the exact local derivative map $A_e$ at the exact real-arithmetic primal values. Use induced Euclidean operator norms on all edge spaces. Suppose a reverse recurrence instead uses local maps $\\widetilde A_e$ from the same branch satisfying $\\|\\widetilde A_e-A_e\\|\\le\\eta_e$, with exact accumulation arithmetic. For an input block $i$, let $\\mathcal P(i,o)$ be the finite directed paths from that block to output $o$, and order the edges of a path $\\pi=(e_1,\\ldots,e_s)$ from input to output. The exact gradient contribution is the transpose of $A_{e_s}\\cdots A_{e_1}$, and the perturbed recurrence returns the corresponding sum using $\\widetilde A_e$. It obeys\n$$\\|\\widetilde g_i-g_i\\|\\le\\sum_{\\pi\\in\\mathcal P(i,o)}\\sum_{\\ell=1}^s\\eta_{e_\\ell}\\left(\\prod_{r<\\ell}\\|A_{e_r}\\|\\right)\\left(\\prod_{r>\\ell}(\\|A_{e_r}\\|+\\eta_{e_r})\\right).$$\nThe bound covers local Jacobian and derivative-evaluation errors represented by $\\eta_e$. It does not include roundoff in the reverse accumulation additions.",
          "strategy": "direct\n\n**Given:** A finite $C^1$ graph on a fixed strict branch, exact edge Jacobians $A_e$, perturbed edge Jacobians $\\widetilde A_e$, and exact real accumulation of the perturbed reverse recurrence.\n\n[F1] [[def-finite-straight-line-computational-program]]: a graph is finite and acyclic, and each parent slot defines a separate edge with a local derivative map.\n\n[F2] [[thm-reverse-mode-computes-gradient]]: exact reverse accumulation returns the gradient of the graph's scalar output.\n\n[F3] [[lem-composition-operator-norm-inequality]]: induced operator norms satisfy $\\|ST\\|\\le\\|S\\|\\,\\|T\\|$ for composable bounded linear maps.\n\n[F4] [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space]]: Euclidean inner products satisfy $|\\langle x,y\\rangle|\\le\\|x\\|_2\\|y\\|_2$.\n\n[A1] Each edge perturbation satisfies the stated operator-norm bound; all sums and products in this theorem are exact real arithmetic.",
