        {
          "id": "lem-certified-linear-solve-jacobian-and-pullback-error",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Certified inverse defects and primal residuals bound linear-solve Jacobian and pullback errors",
          "deps": [
            "thm-implicit-differentiation-forward-and-adjoint-formulas",
            "lem-rounded-linear-and-normal-residual-certificates",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
            "def-operationwise-real-complex-floating-point-model-with-sqrt-and-fma",
            "def-standard-relative-floating-point-model-and-unit-roundoff",
            "lem-product-of-one-plus-deltas-gives-theta-n",
            "thm-singular-value-decomposition",
            "lem-composition-operator-norm-inequality",
            "def-operator-norm"
          ],
          "statement": "For the real mathematical solve primitive $\\phi(A,b)=A^{-1}b$, let $A,B\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$, $b,\\widehat x\\in\\mathbb R^n$, $n\\ge1$, and suppose certified exact-residual bounds satisfy\n$$\\|I-AB\\|_2\\le d<1,\\qquad \\|b-A\\widehat x\\|_2\\le\\rho,\\qquad \\|B\\|_2\\le M,\\qquad K=\\frac{M}{1-d}.$$\nThen $A$ is invertible; writing $x=A^{-1}b$, the fixed linear derivative approximations $\\widetilde J_bh=Bh$ and $\\widetilde J_AH=-BH\\widehat x$ obey\n$$\\|\\widetilde J_b-J_b\\|\\le\\eta_b=Kd,\\qquad \\|\\widetilde J_A-J_A\\|\\le\\eta_A=Kd\\|\\widehat x\\|_2+K^2\\rho.$$\nUse Euclidean vector norms and Frobenius matrix-input norm. For any stored matrix $B$ and stored cotangent $c\\in\\mathbb R^n$, the exact adjoints are $B^Tc$ and $-(B^Tc)\\widehat x^T$. Let $u$ be the unit roundoff in [[def-standard-relative-floating-point-model-and-unit-roundoff]], and assume $nu<1$. For each $j=1,\\ldots,n$, compute $\\widehat\\lambda_j$ by the sequential real dot rule: $p_i=\\operatorname{fl}(B_{ij}c_i)$ for $i=1,\\ldots,n$, $s_1=p_1$, and $s_i=\\operatorname{fl}(s_{i-1}+p_i)$ for $i=2,\\ldots,n$, with each product and addition rounded separately; set $\\widehat\\lambda_j=s_n$. Form the matrix cotangent as the componentwise rounded outer product $-\\operatorname{fl}(\\widehat\\lambda\\widehat x^T)$. Assume every operation succeeds in the operationwise model [[def-operationwise-real-complex-floating-point-model-with-sqrt-and-fma]]: each exact intermediate is zero or normal and every rounded output is finite. Put $\\gamma_n=nu/(1-nu)$ and $t=\\gamma_n\\|B\\|_F$. The resulting cotangent errors are bounded by $(\\eta_b+t)\\|c\\|_2$ and $[\\eta_A+(t+u(M+t))\\|\\widehat x\\|_2]\\|c\\|_2$, respectively. These are derivative approximations to the mathematical solve map, not derivatives of the rounded program.\n\nThe fixed $B$ may be assembled from stored basis-vector solves using a triangular, Cholesky or QR backend; its defect must be certified rather than inferred from a factor residual. Replay must preserve the same $\\widehat x,B$ and strict graph branch. Reverse accumulation additions are excluded.",
          "strategy": "1.1 For every $z$, $\\|ABz\\|\\ge(1-d)\\|z\\|$ by [F9]. Applying [F3] to the square map $AB$, a zero singular value would give a nonzero right singular vector in its kernel, contradicting this lower bound; thus $AB$ is invertible. Then $B$ is injective and $A$ is surjective. The square SVDs of $B$ and $A$ therefore also have no zero singular values, so [F3] makes both invertible. Applying the lower bound to $z=(AB)^{-1}y$ gives $\\|(AB)^{-1}\\|\\le1/(1-d)$. Since $A^{-1}=B(AB)^{-1}$, [F8] gives $\\|A^{-1}\\|\\le K$. The identity $B-A^{-1}=A^{-1}(AB-I)$ gives $\\|B-A^{-1}\\|\\le Kd$, while $\\widehat x-x=A^{-1}(A\\widehat x-b)$ gives $\\|\\widehat x-x\\|\\le K\\rho$. The residuals in these identities are exact; [F2] explains how to certify them from rounded evaluations. [given, F2, F3, F8, F9, algebra]\n\n2.1 Apply [F1] at the exact point $(A,b)$. For the matrix edge, $BH\\widehat x-A^{-1}Hx=(B-A^{-1})H\\widehat x+A^{-1}H(\\widehat x-x)$. Since $\\|H\\|_2\\le\\|H\\|_F$ by [F7], submultiplicativity [F8], the operator action bound [F9], and step 1.1 give $\\eta_A$; the vector edge gives $\\eta_b$. For any linear map $J$ between Euclidean spaces, $\\|J^Ty\\|=\\sup_{\\|x\\|=1}|\\langle J^Ty,x\\rangle|=\\sup_{\\|x\\|=1}|\\langle y,Jx\\rangle|\\le\\|y\\|\\,\\|J\\|$ by [F7], so $\\|J^T\\|\\le\\|J\\|$; applying this to $J^T$ gives equality. Thus the adjoint discrepancies have the same norm bounds. This explicitly accounts for the perturbed primal evaluation point through $\\rho$. [F1, F7, F8, F9, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Fix an output coordinate $j$ of $\\widehat\\lambda$ and put $q_i=B_{ij}c_i$. By success in [F4] and the relative bound in [F5], $p_i=q_i(1+\\alpha_i)$ with $|\\alpha_i|\\le u$; exact-zero products have $\\alpha_i=0$. For each addition $i=2,\\ldots,n$, write $s_i=(s_{i-1}+p_i)(1+\\beta_i)$ with $|\\beta_i|\\le u$, taking $\\beta_i=0$ when the exact sum is zero. Expanding gives $\\widehat\\lambda_j=s_n=\\sum_{i=1}^n q_i(1+\\alpha_i)\\prod_{k=\\max(2,i)}^n(1+\\beta_k)$, with an empty product interpreted as $1$. Each coefficient has at most $n$ factors: for $i=1$ it has one product factor and $n-1$ addition factors, while for $i\\ge2$ it has one product factor and $n-i+1$ addition factors. Thus [F6], $nu<1$, and monotonicity of $ku/(1-ku)$ imply that each coefficient differs from $1$ by at most $\\gamma_n$. Hence $|\\widehat\\lambda_j-(B^Tc)_j|\\le\\gamma_n\\sum_i|B_{ij}||c_i|$. Stacking these bounds and applying [F7] to $|B|^T|c|$ gives $\\|\\widehat\\lambda-B^Tc\\|_2\\le t\\|c\\|_2$. By the adjoint norm equality in step 2.1 and the action bound [F9], $\\|B^Tc\\|_2\\le M\\|c\\|_2$, so $\\|\\widehat\\lambda\\|_2\\le(M+t)\\|c\\|_2$. Each rounded outer-product entry has error at most $u|\\widehat\\lambda_i\\widehat x_j|$, so its Frobenius error is at most $u(M+t)\\|\\widehat x\\|_2\\|c\\|_2$. Combining these bounds with step 2.1 gives the stated vector and matrix cotangent estimates. [given, F4, F5, F6, F7, F9, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 For each realized nonzero $c$, any cotangent error vector $z$ is the action on $c$ of the linear map $zc^T/\\|c\\|^2$, whose induced norm is $\\|z\\|/\\|c\\|$ by Cauchy--Schwarz [F7] and equality on $c/\\|c\\|$ [F9]; vectorize the matrix cotangent for the matrix edge. When $c=0$, successful operations return exact zero. Thus the realized rounded pullbacks admit local linear-map perturbations with the displayed bounds, without asserting global linearity of rounding. The graph perturbation theorem may propagate these realized maps with exact accumulation. [F7, F9, step 3.1, algebra, construct] ∎",
          "sources": [
