      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-diagonal-lower-upper-splitting-convention",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Diagonal, lower, and upper parts in the stationary-iteration convention",
          "deps": [
            "def-triangular-and-diagonal-matrices-over-a-commutative-ring"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "statement": "Let $F$ be either $\\mathbb R$ or $\\mathbb C$, let $n\\ge1$, fix the coordinate\norder $1,\\ldots,n$, and let $A=(a_{ij})\\in M_n(F)$. Define $D,L,U\\in M_n(F)$\nby\n\n$$D_{ij}=\\begin{cases}a_{ii}&i=j,\\\\0&i\\ne j,\\end{cases}\\qquad\nL_{ij}=\\begin{cases}-a_{ij}&i>j,\\\\0&i\\le j,\\end{cases}\\qquad\nU_{ij}=\\begin{cases}-a_{ij}&i<j,\\\\0&i\\ge j.\\end{cases}$$\n\nThus $D$ is diagonal, $L$ is strictly lower triangular, $U$ is strictly upper\ntriangular (in the sense of\n[[def-triangular-and-diagonal-matrices-over-a-commutative-ring]]), and\n$A=D-L-U$. If $A=A^*$, then $U=L^*$, where $*$ denotes conjugate transpose.\nThe decomposition uses the fixed coordinate order and is an exact-arithmetic\nidentity.\n",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Yousef Saad, Iterative Methods for Sparse Linear Systems, 2nd ed., §4.1",
                "url": "https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~saad/IterMethBook_2ndEd.pdf",
                "locator": "Printed pp. 105–108: the diagonal/strict lower/strict upper decomposition used for Jacobi, Gauss–Seidel, and SOR."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Bindel, Matrix Computations (CS 6210), Cornell University, Fall 2012, Lecture 31",
                "url": "https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~bindel/class/cs6210-f12/notes/lec31.pdf",
                "locator": "PDF pp. 3–4: the fixed-order lower/upper splitting used for Gauss–Seidel and SOR."
