          "justified_by": [],
          "statement": "Fix an interval $[a,b]$ and an open set $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ on which each exact IVP flow $\\phi(t;s)$ exists for all $t\\in[a,b]$. A **certified differentiable IVP evaluator** on $U$ declares a $C^1$ approximate endpoint map (here $C^1$ means total differentiability with continuous derivative entries) $\\widetilde\\Phi:U\\to\\mathbb R^d$ and a stated number $\\delta_{\\rm IVP}\\ge0$. A call at $s\\in U$ either returns failure or supplies a continuous approximate trajectory $\\widetilde\\phi(\\cdot;s)$, the endpoint $\\widetilde\\Phi(s)$ and the exact derivative $D\\widetilde\\Phi(s)$ for that numerical map, satisfying\n\n$$\\sup_{t\\in[a,b]}\\|\\widetilde\\phi(t;s)-\\phi(t;s)\\|_2\\le\\delta_{\\rm IVP},\\qquad \\widetilde\\Phi(s)=\\widetilde\\phi(b;s).$$\n\nThe same $\\delta_{\\rm IVP}$ bounds every successful call, uniformly in its initial state; no approximate trajectory or error bound is required at a failed call. The declared endpoint map remains $C^1$ on $U$ even when some calls fail. The returned derivative is not asserted to equal the derivative of the exact flow. This is an interface contract for a supplied method; it does not assert that any particular integrator satisfies the contract.",
