      "description": "Controlling repaired prose: research/plan-numerical-dynamics-track.md, pair P-NUM-ODE-02, and its exact incorporated mathematical-contract appendix. Read the complete repaired contract, preserved scope/amendments/examples, and actual supplier restrictions before authoring. Canonical statistics/linear algebra methods remain at their published homes; only new numerical variants/results and missing typed prerequisites are authored here.\n\n{\"scope\": \"# Independent numerical ODE prerequisite pairs — intake proposal\\n\\nDraft proposal only. No ML consumer is a prerequisite. All procedures and proofs remain to be authored.\\n\\n### P-NUM-ODE-01 — One-step ODE consistency and local error\\n\\n**Pages:** one-step-ode-consistency-and-local-error / one-step-ode-consistency-and-local-error-examples. **Scope:** Define a one-step method $\\\\Psi_h(t,y)$, local defect $\\\\|\\\\Psi_h(t,y(t))-y(t+h)\\\\|$, and stability. For a fixed interval $[0,T]$ and exact solutions staying in a compact tube, state the order-$p$ hypothesis as a uniform local defect $\\\\le Ch^{p+1}$ and the one-step stability bound $\\\\|\\\\Psi_h(t,u)-\\\\Psi_h(t,v)\\\\|\\\\le(1+Lh)\\\\|u-v\\\\|$. Derive these premises for Euler when $f$ is $C^1$ in time and state with bounded derivatives on that tube; any named Runge–Kutta method must state its order conditions and required derivatives separately. **Proof route:** Taylor-expand the exact flow and the selected method increment, bound remainders uniformly on the tube, and prove the stated stability estimate; do not infer an order from the method label alone. **Examples:** Euler on a scalar linear field; a nonsmooth field where its local order drops. **Requires:** thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution, thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives, and a proved Taylor remainder estimate; the local error calculation is explicit, but a numerical-analysis source remains unaudited. **Sources:** Chen et al., neural-ODE Appendix B/C and numerical issues, PDF pp. 13–16, and Papamakarios et al., continuous-flow section pp. 31–33, discuss solver use but do not prove the error theorem. Acquire/read a matching numerical-ODE source before publication. This pair precedes P-NUM-ODE-02.\\n\\n### P-NUM-ODE-02 — Global ODE and log-density integration error\\n\\n**Pages:** global-ode-and-density-error / global-ode-and-density-error-examples. **Scope:** Under P-NUM-ODE-01’s uniform order-$p$ defect/stability bounds and a fixed finite interval, derive $\\\\max_n\\\\|y_n-y(t_n)\\\\|\\\\le C_T h^p$ by the discrete Grönwall recurrence. For a continuous normalizing flow augment the state by $\\\\ell'=-\\\\operatorname{div}f(x,t)$ and bound the log-density error separately. For Euler, $f\\\\in C^2$ with bounded derivatives on a compact tube makes the augmented vector field locally Lipschitz and supports a local $O(h^2)$ defect; for order $p>1$, require the method-specific order conditions and enough bounded derivatives of the augmented field, or state a separate quadrature theorem with matching smoothness. $C^2$ regularity alone does not justify arbitrary order-$p$ convergence. State solver, quadrature, and state-error contributions separately. A state trajectory error alone does not control log-density or adjoint-gradient error. **Proof route:** derive $e_{n+1}\\\\le(1+Lh)e_n+Ch^{p+1}$, iterate it, then apply a second bound to the augmented/log-density component under its own local defect and stability estimates. **Examples:** Euler global error for a linear flow; same state solver with a separately refined density quadrature. **Requires:** P-NUM-ODE-01 — One-step ODE consistency and local error; discrete Grönwall and the divergence-Lipschitz estimate, proved locally if no exact supplier exists. **Sources:** Chen et al. pp. 13–16 and Papamakarios et al. pp. 31–33 discuss solver use and numerical issues but do not prove these error theorems; the local defect/stability/Grönwall proof remains a clearly specified authoring obligation. ML-58 — Neural ODEs and continuous normalizing flows and ML-61 — Conditional flow matching and transport paths consume this only when numerical error is claimed.\\n\\n\\n## Binding consumed contract\\n\\n**P-NUM-ODE-01/02.** Fix d≥1, T>0, h=T/N, N≥1, a common open time-state domain and a compact solution tube with positive margin. Use Euclidean vector norms and induced operator norms. The exact IVP solution y and every numerical stage/state in the theorem stay in that tube. A one-step map Ψ_h(t,·) is defined there and satisfies, for constants C,L≥0 independent of N and j, ||Ψ_h(t_j,y(t_j))−y(t_(j+1))||≤C h^(p+1) and ||Ψ_h(t_j,v)−Ψ_h(t_j,w)||≤(1+Lh)||v−w||. With initial error e₀≤C₀h^p, prove max_j e_j≤(C₀e^(LT)+C(e^(LT)−1)/L)h^p for L>0, and (C₀+CT)h^p for L=0. Derive Euler's C and L from f∈C¹ with finite field, time-derivative and state-Jacobian bounds on the tube; derive each higher-order tableau's defect separately from its exact derivative/order conditions. For augmented log density ℓ′=−div f, Euler requires f∈C² with bounded relevant derivatives of f and div f, the augmented tube, and a separate state/log-defect estimate; no arbitrary higher order follows from C². Local proof route: Taylor/integral remainder, invariant-tube admissibility and geometric-sum recurrence, using actual root ODE/Taylor/Grönwall suppliers. Matching source design: Hairer–Wanner Ch. III §§III.2–III.5, PDF pp. 4–11/printed 60–67, as recorded by the recovery packet. NA-15 supplies concrete conditions to instantiate the abstract theorem, never a prerequisite of the generic theorem. Consumers: NA-15, NA-18 notation only, NA-21 when numerical shooting is quantified, NA-45 matching ODE terms.\\n\\n\\nThe abstract recurrence/global-error theorem is owned by P-NUM-ODE-02; P-NUM-ODE-01 owns local defect/stability definitions and Euler derivation. NA-15 owns concrete higher-order tableaux. The recurrence accepts hypotheses directly and must never depend back on NA-15.\\n\", \"pro