          "dependency_level": 2,
          "statement": "Fix a constant-step $k$-step method from [[def-linear-multistep-method-consistency-order-and-root-condition]] on $[0,T]$. Convergence means that for every admitted first-order IVP and every sequence of starting values whose errors at $t_0,\\ldots,t_{k-1}$ tend to zero as $h=T/N\\to0$, the maximum grid error tends to zero. For exact solutions with two continuous derivatives, choose a compact state set $K\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ with the exact trajectory in its interior and suppose $f$ is Lipschitz in the state on $[0,T]\\times K$. For an implicit method, require a unique selected root in $K$ for every history in $K$. The method converges for every such IVP if and only if it is consistent and satisfies the root condition. More precisely, if its order is at least $p\\ge1$, the exact solution has $p+1$ continuous derivatives, and the starting errors are $O(h^p)$, then its global error is $O(h^p)$.\n\nFor the sufficiency and order estimates, the branch assumption means that a unique selected root exists in $K$ for every history in $K$; the error proof then keeps the computed histories there. Convergence is tested on defined runs. In the necessity tests $y'=0$ and $y'=1$, the implicit equation has a unique root in all of $\\mathbb R^d$ at every step because $\\alpha_k\\ne0$ and the right-hand side is independent of the new state, so those tests remain defined even if an unstable mode leaves the sufficiency tube. The global estimate does not assert existence of an implicit root for arbitrary IVPs or step sizes.",
