          "statement": "Let d>=1 and let C be a nonempty closed convex subset of R^d, closed in the standard Euclidean metric topology of [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] and convex as in [[def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space]]. Use the one-based coordinate labels $z_j:=z(j-1)$ for $1\\le j\\le d$ for the vectors of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Fix x_1 in C, a constant eta>0, and epsilon>0. An admissible input consists of convex losses ell_t:C->R and a subgradient oracle that returns a finite vector g_t in partial ell_t(x_t) at each queried iterate, with subgradient convention [[def-subgradient-and-subdifferential]]. On round t, observe ell_t and obtain g_t from this oracle. For j=1,...,d set\n\n$$s_{t,j}=\\sqrt{\\epsilon^2+\\sum_{k=1}^t g_{k,j}^2},\\qquad A_t=\\operatorname{diag}(s_{t,1},...,s_{t,d}).$$\n\nFor a positive diagonal matrix A, write ||z||_A^2=z^TAz. Set x_{t+1} to the unique minimizer\n\n$$x_{t+1}=\\operatorname*{argmin}_{x\\in C}\\{\\eta\\langle g_t,x\\rangle+\\tfrac12\\|x-x_t\\|_{A_t}^2\\}.$$\n\nBecause epsilon>0, every diagonal entry s_{t,j} is positive. The displayed minimizer exists and is unique by [[lem-diagonal-adagrad-subproblem-well-defined]], so the procedure is defined at each finite round. This is the proximal update in the diagonal metric A_t; after the coordinate scaling A_t^{1/2}, its subproblem is an ordinary Euclidean proximal problem in the sense of [[def-proximal-mapping]].\n\n\n",
