          "id": "def-bounded-lipschitz-dual-norm-for-signed-measures",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Bounded-Lipschitz dual discrepancy for finite signed measures",
          "statement": "Let $\\omega,\\nu$ be finite signed Borel measures on $\\mathbb R^d$ such that $|\\omega-\\nu|(\\mathbb R^d)<\\infty$. For each bounded Lipschitz test function, integration against $\\omega-\\nu$ is defined by [[def-integration-against-a-signed-or-complex-measure]]; boundedness and finite total variation make every test in the following set integrable. Set $\\|\\phi\\|_\\infty:=\\sup_x|\\phi(x)|$ and $\\operatorname{Lip}(\\phi):=\\sup_{x\\ne y}|\\phi(x)-\\phi(y)|/|x-y|$, with $\\operatorname{Lip}(\\phi)=0$ when $d=0$, using the Euclidean norm from [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Define the bounded-Lipschitz dual discrepancy\n$$d_{BL}(\\omega,\\nu):=\\sup\\left\\{\\left|\\int\\phi\\,d(\\omega-\\nu)\\right|:\\|\\phi\\|_\\infty\\le1,\\ \\operatorname{Lip}(\\phi)\\le1\\right\\}.$$\nThe integral bound by total variation shows this supremum is finite. This page uses only the displayed dual quantity and makes no separate metric-separation claim. No Jordan decomposition, positivity, or probability normalization is needed.",
