          "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$, with the Euclidean norm from [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. An interaction kernel is a continuous map $K:\\mathbb R^d\\times\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^d$. It is globally Lipschitz with constant $L\\ge0$ in the convention used on this page if\n$$|K(x,y)-K(x',y')|\\le L(|x-x'|+|y-y'|)$$\nfor all $x,y,x',y'\\in\\mathbb R^d$. This implies the linear-growth estimate\n$$|K(x,y)|\\le |K(0,0)|+L(|x|+|y|).$$\nThe notation $L$ always refers to this two-variable convention; the stability factor below is therefore $2L$.",
