          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Completeness of the weighted continuous trajectory space",
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $(E,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a probability space, $T>0$, and $d\\ge1$. Use the Euclidean norm of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Let $\\mathcal X$ be the space, modulo equality $\\mu$-almost everywhere, of jointly measurable maps $X:[0,T]\\times E\\to\\mathbb R^d$ whose paths are continuous for almost every label and whose time-supremum function is measurable, and for which\n$$\\|X\\|_{\\mathcal X}:=\\int_E\\sup_{0\\le t\\le T}|X_t(z)|d\\mu(z)<\\infty.$$\nIn this formula and in $\\|X-Y\\|_{\\mathcal X}$, take the supremum on a measurable conull set where the relevant paths are continuous and extend it by zero off that set; equality almost everywhere means equality of whole paths outside one measurable null set. The seminorm identifies maps equal almost everywhere, and the induced distance $d_{\\mathcal X}(X,Y)=\\|X-Y\\|_{\\mathcal X}$ is a metric on the quotient; it is complete.",
